ICMP echo reply table and reply builder
=======================================

Small table of outstanding ping requests so `ping`-style
programs can wait for a matching ECHO_REP. When the inet RX
path sees an ECHO_REP it looks up `(id, seq)` here and stamps
`reply_ms` + `src_ip`; the client polls via
`INET_CMD_ICMP_CHECK`. The companion `inet_send_icmp_echo_reply`
builds the outbound reply frame for an inbound ECHO_REQ.

Phase 2 chunk 3 added per-tid cleanup so a dying client doesn't
leak a slot. Bounded table (INET_PING_MAX = 4) keeps the worst
case predictable in a single-threaded server.

Same `oskit.servers` module as `inet_proto.lsysl`, so sibling
helpers (`inet_frame_alloc` / `_buf` / `_release` from
`frame_pool.lsysl`, `inet_nic_send_packet` from the nic-client
wrappers) resolve without imports.

    module oskit.servers

    import std.net.{ICMP_ECHO_REP, pkt_write_mac, pkt_write_u16,
        net_checksum}


### Ping reply table

Small table of outstanding ping requests so `ping`-style programs
can wait for the matching ECHO_REP. When the inet RX path sees an
ECHO_REP, it looks up (id, seq) here and stamps reply_ms +
src_ip. The client polls via INET_CMD_ICMP_CHECK.

    val INET_PING_MAX = 4

    struct InetPingSlot
        in_use: int
        owner_tid: int
        id: int
        seq: int
        send_ms: int
        src_ip: u32
        reply_ms: int
        received: int

    var inet_ping_table: [4]InetPingSlot


Allocate a slot and record (id, seq, send_ms). Returns slot
index on success, -1 if the table is full. Duplicate (id, seq)
from a previous un-checked slot just overwrites that slot.

    inet_ping_register(owner_tid: int, id: int, seq: int, send_ms: int) -> int
        var found = -1
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PING_MAX
            if inet_ping_table[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_ping_table[i].id == id
                    if inet_ping_table[i].seq == seq
                        found = i
            i += 1
        if found < 0
            i = 0
            while i < INET_PING_MAX
                if inet_ping_table[i].in_use == 0
                    found = i
                    i = INET_PING_MAX
                else
                    i += 1
        if found < 0
            return -1
        inet_ping_table[found].in_use = 1
        inet_ping_table[found].owner_tid = owner_tid
        inet_ping_table[found].id = id
        inet_ping_table[found].seq = seq
        inet_ping_table[found].send_ms = send_ms
        inet_ping_table[found].src_ip = u32(0)
        inet_ping_table[found].reply_ms = 0
        inet_ping_table[found].received = 0
        found


Phase 2 chunk 3 — PID_EXIT cleanup audit. When a process dies
without reading its ping reply, the slot would otherwise leak
until id+seq collide or a peer sends an unsolicited reply.
INET_PING_MAX is only 4, so this becomes a real pinch point
under any kind of churn. Walk the table and free every slot
whose owner_tid matches.

    inet_ping_cleanup_owner(tid: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PING_MAX
            if inet_ping_table[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_ping_table[i].owner_tid == tid
                    inet_ping_table[i].in_use = 0
            i += 1


Selftest helper — used by INET_CMD_PID_EXIT_SELFTEST. Registers
a synthetic ping owned by `tid`, then verifies cleanup_owner
frees it. Returns 0 on pass, 1..3 on failure step.

    inet_ping_cleanup_selftest(tid: int) -> int
        val before = inet_ping_count_owner(tid)
        if before != 0
            return 1
        if inet_ping_register(tid, 0xdead, 0xbeef, 0) < 0
            return 2
        if inet_ping_count_owner(tid) != 1
            return 3
        inet_ping_cleanup_owner(tid)
        if inet_ping_count_owner(tid) != 0
            return 4
        0


Count in-use ping slots whose owner_tid matches `tid`.  Used
above and from the selftest harness.

    inet_ping_count_owner(tid: int) -> int
        var n = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PING_MAX
            if inet_ping_table[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_ping_table[i].owner_tid == tid
                    n += 1
            i += 1
        n


Called from `inet_handle_frame` on ICMP_ECHO_REP. Updates the
matching slot with src_ip and reply_ms so the client's CHECK
returns a completed entry.

    inet_ping_deliver(src_ip: u32, id: int, seq: int, now_ms: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PING_MAX
            if inet_ping_table[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_ping_table[i].received == 0
                    if inet_ping_table[i].id == id
                        if inet_ping_table[i].seq == seq
                            inet_ping_table[i].src_ip = src_ip
                            inet_ping_table[i].reply_ms = now_ms
                            inet_ping_table[i].received = 1
                            return
            i += 1


Look up (id, seq) and, if a reply has arrived, fill `out_src_ip`
+ `out_rtt` and free the slot. Returns 1 on complete, 0 on still
waiting, -1 on no such slot.

    inet_ping_check(id: int, seq: int, out_src_ip: *u32, out_rtt: *int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_PING_MAX
            if inet_ping_table[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_ping_table[i].id == id
                    if inet_ping_table[i].seq == seq
                        if inet_ping_table[i].received != 0
                            *out_src_ip = inet_ping_table[i].src_ip
                            *out_rtt = inet_ping_table[i].reply_ms - inet_ping_table[i].send_ms
                            inet_ping_table[i].in_use = 0
                            return 1
                        return 0
            i += 1
        -1


ICMP echo reply in response to `req`. `req` points at the start
of the Ethernet frame (ICMP payload starts at offset 14 + ihl).

    inet_send_icmp_reply(req: *byte, total_len: int) -> int
        if inet_our_mac_known == 0
            return -1
        if total_len > INET_FRAME_BUF_SIZE
            return -1
        if total_len < 14 + 20 + 8
            return -1
        val fidx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val frame = inet_frame_buf(fidx)

Copy the whole request into our buffer as a starting point; we
then rewrite the bits that change between request and reply.

        var i = 0
        while i < total_len
            frame[i] = req[i]
            i += 1

Ethernet: swap src and dst.

        pkt_write_mac(frame, 0, req + 6)
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 6, inet_our_mac)

IPv4: swap src and dst IP, zero checksum, recompute.

        var j = 0
        while j < 4
            frame[14 + 12 + j] = req[14 + 16 + j]   // dst <- old src
            frame[14 + 16 + j] = req[14 + 12 + j]   // src <- old dst
            j += 1
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 14 + 10, 0)
        val ihl_bytes = (int(frame[14]) & 0x0f) * 4
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 14 + 10, net_checksum(frame + 14, ihl_bytes))

ICMP: flip type from echo-request to echo-reply, zero checksum,
recompute over the full ICMP message.

        val icmp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
        frame[icmp_off] = byte(ICMP_ECHO_REP)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, icmp_off + 2, 0)
        val icmp_len = total_len - icmp_off
        pkt_write_u16(frame, icmp_off + 2,
            net_checksum(frame + icmp_off, icmp_len))

        val rc = inet_nic_send_packet(frame, total_len)
        inet_frame_release(fidx)
        rc
